Calculus and differential-equation preparation — Strang and Herman, OpenStax (@ic-calculus: 0, is #reference, #part-of @ic-sources) { r_027WKVM5NG5X84M86TSVRA77J3
Gilbert Strang and Edwin “Jed” Herman. Calculus, Volumes 1 and 2, OpenStax, originally published 30 March 2016, with later revisions. [Volume 1](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/1-introduction) · [Volume 2](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-2/pages/1-introduction) · [Differential equations, Volume 2 chapter 4](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-2/pages/4-introduction).

Use the complete relevant limits, differentiation, integration and application chapters of Volume 1, then the entire differential-equation chapter of Volume 2 for changing-inventory models. Work the examples and exercises independently; read whole volumes for a fuller mathematical foundation where needed. The chemical-model Record supplies the connecting meanings rather than duplicating an entire calculus course.

Author/date, chapter structure and current notices were checked on 29 October 2026. The current web/PDF notices identify CC BY-NC-SA 4.0; some older PDFs retain CC BY 4.0. Preserve the actual version and its attribution/component terms rather than silently treating every edition as having identical rights. The book pages, figures and assessment material remain external links.

**Return:** [Industrial chemistry sources — whole books, courses, technical methods and primary evidence](../Sources.lingua#r_3084780FHMK499ZDAW07TYA3TQ).

**Third mathematics-core inspection — 31 October 2026:** rechecked the current Volume 1 [preface and citation/rights notice](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/preface): Strang and Herman, original publication 30 March 2016, and current CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 notice. Selected definitions, arguments and hypotheses were inspected in sections [2.2](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/2-2-the-limit-of-a-function), [2.3](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/2-3-the-limit-laws), [2.4](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/2-4-continuity), [2.5](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/2-5-the-precise-definition-of-a-limit), [3.1](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/3-1-defining-the-derivative), [3.2](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/3-2-the-derivative-as-a-function), [3.3](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/3-3-differentiation-rules), [3.6](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/3-6-the-chain-rule), [4.2](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/4-2-linear-approximations-and-differentials), [4.3](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/4-3-maxima-and-minima), [4.4](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/4-4-the-mean-value-theorem), [5.1](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/5-1-approximating-areas), [5.2](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/5-2-the-definite-integral) and [5.3](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/5-3-the-fundamental-theorem-of-calculus). The larger original assignments remain; no whole-volume, exercise or interactive-media audit is claimed.

For this common block, work the limit, continuity, polynomial derivative, approximation, partition and fundamental-theorem material with the stated prerequisites. Transcendental examples, general integration techniques and multivariable/differential-equation work remain later formation. The new chain-rule argument handles zero inner increments, which the source’s explicitly informal proof sets aside. Retain the continuity hypothesis in Theorem 5.4 rather than extending nearby prose about antiderivatives to every integrable function; the original common-core step-function counterexample explains that limit. General extreme-value and continuous-integrability results are identified as used foundations, while the new polynomial examples include direct proofs and independent checks. The teaching study and programs are original; source text, figures, assessments and media remain external.


**Third chemistry-core mathematical preparation — 31 October 2026:** inspected selected definitions and arguments in Volume 2 [2.7, integrals, exponential functions and logarithms](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-2/pages/2-7-integrals-exponential-functions-and-logarithms), and rechecked its [preface/current CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 notice](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-2/pages/preface). The first attempted 1.6 URL was incorrect; 2.7 is the integral-based construction used here. The common bridge establishes ln’s positive domain and unbounded range before defining exp, and supplies a difference-quotient justification of inverse differentiability before using that derivative. General integration techniques and the complete differential-equation chapter remain later assignments. The original rational brackets and initial-value/observation study supplement the reading; no complete section-exercise or volume audit is claimed. Earlier assignments, source identity and component notices remain intact.


**Fourth mathematics-core inspection — 31 October 2026:** rechecked selected Volume 1 [2.3 limit/squeeze context](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/2-3-the-limit-laws) and [3.5 trigonometric derivatives](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/3-5-derivatives-of-trigonometric-functions), including the two small-angle limits used by its derivative proof and current rights notice. The common lessons supply their own geometric premise and two-sided limits before differentiating. Add Gilbert Strang and Edwin Herman's [Calculus Volume 3, section 7.1](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/7-1-second-order-linear-equations) for second-order equations and initial/boundary data; its [preface](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/preface) confirms the authors. Selected complex-root and initial/boundary passages were inspected. General characteristic equations and Euler's complex-exponential development retain later preparation; the new undamped initial-value proof instead uses verified trigonometric derivatives and a constant nonnegative quadratic. This supplements the existing Volumes 1–2 reading without replacing their assignments. No whole-volume or exercise audit is claimed, and media remain external. A candidate MIT characteristic-equation URL was inaccessible and is not claimed inspected.


**Sixth mathematics-core inspection — 1 November 2026:** rechecked the current Volume 3 [preface and CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 notice](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/preface). Selected definitions and hypotheses were inspected in [4.1 functions](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/4-1-functions-of-several-variables), [4.2 limits](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/4-2-limits-and-continuity), [4.3 partials](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/4-3-partial-derivatives), [4.4 differentiability](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/4-4-tangent-planes-and-linear-approximations), [4.5 composition](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/4-5-the-chain-rule), [4.6 gradients](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/4-6-directional-derivatives-and-the-gradient), [4.7 extrema](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/4-7-maxima-minima-problems) and [4.8 multipliers](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/4-8-lagrange-multipliers). Retain differentiability in the gradient theorem: a proof sentence in 4.6 incorrectly infers it merely from partial existence. The common counterexample demonstrates the distinction. The common chain proof uses an absolute bound and handles zero inner increments. The regularity premise of 4.8 is retained; general level-set existence is identified as later analysis. Checkpoint 4.22 was independently recalculated: differential 9/50, finite change 90341/500000, remainder 341/500000. This is one bounded published exercise, not a whole-section audit. Complete chapters and other exercises remain assignments; source media stay external and earlier version/component rights remain.


**Eighth mathematics-core inspection — 1 November 2026:** inspected Volume 2 [5.1 sequences](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-2/pages/5-1-sequences), including its monotone-convergence statement, [5.2 infinite series](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-2/pages/5-2-infinite-series), including partial sums, geometric cancellation, telescoping and Example 5.9(1), and [6.3 Taylor series](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-2/pages/6-3-taylor-and-maclaurin-series), including the distinction between a convergent series and a representation and the remainder theorem. Rechecked the Volume 2 [preface/current CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 notice](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-2/pages/preface). The common block proves its monotone/Cauchy claims from an explicit real-completeness premise, rather than treating the source's unproved monotone theorem as a numerical observation. It retains sufficient continuous derivatives through order n+1 in the finite Taylor identity; nearby informal prose mentioning only n derivatives is insufficient for a derivative-(n+1) remainder. The selected geometric example is reconstructed by exact finite summation and a signed remainder. Whole chapters, exercises and later volume assignments remain broader learner work. Existing source identity, assignments and component rights are preserved; no media are bundled.

**Common oriented-field inspection — 1 November 2026:** selected Volume 3 [5.1](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/5-1-double-integrals-over-rectangular-regions) Riemann-sum definition, [6.7](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/6-7-stokes-theorem) Theorem 6.19 orientation/regularity and [6.8](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/6-8-the-divergence-theorem) Theorem 6.20 and informal box explanation were read. The common block proves rectangle, box and C² parameter-patch cases; broader curved-domain statements are explicitly accepted. The [preface](https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/preface) rechecks Strang/Herman, original 30 March 2016 publication, current CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 and separate component/reuse notices. General chapters and exercises remain assignments. No source text, figures, assessments or media were copied; source identity and earlier rights qualifications are preserved.

